In the name of God, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful

# Project
- Factory security app. The full spec is in docs/technical-spec.md (Part D = Android architecture, Part B11 = server events).
- The Android project is in android/. Run Gradle from that directory.
- Display name Herz. Application id and namespace: com.raghim.herz. Do not change them.
- Kotlin only, Jetpack Compose, Material 3, minSdk 26, MVI with StateFlow.
- Modules: follow the module graph in spec D3. Feature modules must not depend on other feature modules.
- DI: Hilt. Database: Room. Network: Retrofit + OkHttp + kotlinx.serialization.
- Library versions are only in android/gradle/libs.versions.toml. Never hardcode versions in build files.
- Every new or edited text file starts with: In the name of God, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. Use a comment when the format requires it. See .cursor/rules/opening-line.mdc.

# Workflow
- After every change, from android/, run: ./gradlew :<module>:testDebugUnitTest
- Before finishing a task, from android/, run: ./gradlew assembleDebug lint
- After the dev flavor exists, use testDevDebugUnitTest and assembleDevDebug instead.
- If a build fails, read the full error, fix the cause, and run again. Do not delete or skip tests to make a build pass.
- Write unit tests for every ViewModel and use case (JUnit + Turbine + fakes in :core:testing).
- Small steps: one module or one feature per task.
- On Windows the wrapper command is gradlew.bat. ./gradlew also works in PowerShell from android/.
- Report honestly what was verified and what was not (for example "not tested with real cameras"). Never claim a check ran if it did not.

# Security (do not simplify)
- Never log tokens, passwords or full URLs with credentials.
- Do not disable certificate pinning or allow cleartext, not even for testing. Use the dev flavor config instead.
- The DeviceKey / BiometricPrompt code must follow spec D11 exactly.

# Reliability (do not skip)
The app runs a wall of many cameras and doors for hours. Design every change for 50 cameras and 1000 taps, not for the demo with 2.
- Owned resources: every socket, ExoPlayer/decoder, MulticastLock, network callback, file and coroutine scope has one owner that closes it, in `finally`, `use`, `awaitClose`, `onDispose` or `release()`.
- Bounded growth: anything that grows with cameras, doors or user actions (players, maps, caches, pending requests, retries) has a cap or a cleanup. Expired or unused entries are removed.
- Heavy media lives only while needed: release players and connections when paused, off screen or in the background (after a short grace period). UI code attaches to an existing player; it never creates one.
- No endless waits: every network call has connect, read and overall timeouts. No visible state such as "Connecting" or "Unlocking" may last forever; a watchdog turns it into an error the user can see.
- Retries: exponential backoff with a cap and jitter, stopped when the work is no longer wanted, and no retry for errors that cannot succeed (wrong login, unsupported format).
- Coroutines: only in an owned scope (viewModelScope, injected @AppScope, or a class scope cancelled in release()). Never GlobalScope. Loops check cancellation.
- Main thread: no blocking I/O and no heavy work in composition. Use the IO or Default dispatcher.
- Limits (live tiles, scan parallelism, buffers, timeouts) are named constants with a comment, taken from the spec where it gives numbers.
- For changes to video, network, discovery or doors: say in the summary what is opened, who closes it, the timeouts, and what happens with many items. Add a unit test for the limit or cleanup when it can run on the JVM.
